Other information such as names of people related to the sample, content of conversation 
held in the sampling field, processes running at the time of sampling (in case the sample was 
taken in a chemical company), eye witness reports and anything that happened during the 
sampling event should be documented and must accompany each sample when forwarded 
for analysis. 
All the information regarding sampling procedures (temperature, humidity and location of 
the scenario, sampling team, res ult of the first response detectors, sample coding , etc.) is 
recorded either manually or electronically before leaving the sampling site . Electronic 
records should be copied, verified, sealed, transported to the storage site and protected from 
electromagnetic fields that could destroy or corrupt the data. 
